  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1057 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge across central and eastern Texas on Friday \nwill begin to shift eastward as the upper-level trough to the west\nstarts to move into the West Central Texas region by Sunday \nmorning. With moistening low-level south to southeasterly flow, \nexpect an increase in cloud cover during the weekend. As a series\nof upper-level disturbances associated with the aforementioned\ntrough swing through the area, expect increasing showers and \nthunderstorm chances Sunday through Tuesday. Shower and \nthunderstorms chances at this time remain in the low to medium \nrange (20-40 percent), with the highest chances across the Big \nCountry Monday night into Tuesday morning. Models continue to show\na cold front tracking through the area on Tuesday, keeping some\nlower (20-30 percent) showers and thunderstorm chances in the\nforecast through Tuesday.\n\nTemperatures will remain overall pleasant and slightly above\nnormal (around 5 degrees above normal), with morning lows in the \nmid 50s to around 60 and afternoon highs in the mid 70s to lower \n80s through Saturday. Then, with increasing cloud cover limiting \ndaytime high temperatures, temperatures will mostly remain in the \n70s through Wednesday. Temperatures look to be a little cooler \nbehind the front in the Big Country on Tuesday.",
